# Spec pipeline

All outputs live under `docs/specs/<feature>/`. Each step reads the previous outputs and writes
its own; never restate content, link to it. Keep documents terse and structured so they generate
from the spec rather than being re-authored. Use synthetic data only in examples.

## Steps

### /eco:intake <feature>
Read every source the user provides (BRD, notes, Figma export/link). Write `intake.md`: each fact
on its own line, each citing its source. Flag contradictions. No interpretation yet.

### /eco:clarify <feature>
Delegate to the `eco-clarify` skill (checklists + live interview). Gate: no blocking question open.

### /eco:spec <feature>
Write `spec.md`, `nfr.md`, `data-catalog.md`.
- `spec.md`: a requirements table. Each row: `REQ-nnn | statement | acceptance criteria`. Allocate
  REQ-IDs sequentially; never reuse or renumber. Record approved assumptions as `ASSUMPTION:` lines.
- `nfr.md`: numeric NFR targets (from the nfr checklist), each tagged with the REQ-IDs it governs.
- `data-catalog.md`: every data field with PII class, purpose, consent basis, retention (dpdp checklist).
Gate: spec sign-off (human).
Before sign-off, run `eco-forge-spec-lint docs/specs/<feature>` — the proactive QA gate. It fails
(exit 2) on blocking requirement defects: ambiguous/non-measurable wording, NFRs without a number,
duplicate REQ-IDs, data fields with no PII classification, missing error paths, and TBD
placeholders. Resolve every blocking defect here; a requirement defect fixed now is far cheaper
than one found by a code gate later. Treat the remaining (non-blocking) findings as review items.

### /eco:design <feature>
Write `hld.md`, `lld.md`, `integration.md`, `api/openapi.yaml`, and ADRs under `docs/adr/`.
Each design section cites the REQ-IDs it realises. Diagrams are Mermaid text, not images.
Gate: design sign-off (human).
Before sign-off, run `eco-forge-design-lint docs/specs/<feature>` — the Layer-1 prevention gate.
It fails (exit 2) on design defects that would otherwise become code defects: a requirement with
no design coverage, a design citing an undeclared requirement, an OpenAPI operation with no error
responses or no security, and a decision with no ADR. Resolve each before construction.

### /eco:plan <feature>
Write `tasks.md`: work items, each citing its REQ-IDs and a definition of done. When GitLab is
configured, push these as issues (via the GitLab MCP) grouped under a milestone.

### /eco:uat <feature>
Write `uat.md`: at least one UAT case per REQ-ID, as Given/When/Then, in business language.

### /eco:export <feature>
Render the documents to branded DOCX/PDF for sign-off (pandoc or the document skills, whichever
the project configured). Record the sign-off on the GitLab issue, not in the document.

## Traceability (enforced, not optional)
After `/eco:uat`, run `eco-forge-trace docs/specs/<feature>`. It fails the build unless every
REQ-ID has at least one test and one UAT case, and there are no orphan references. Fix gaps before
moving on. The generated `trace.md` is the REQ -> design -> task -> test -> UAT view.
